One corpus fault, found by a seventh implementation

The first byte of a part request is the hashmap exhausted flag, and
what the reference does with it is compare it:

    RNS/Resource.py#HASHMAP_IS_EXHAUSTED
        wants_more_hashmap = True if request_data[0] == ... else False

cmd/dump and tools/gen both read the byte as a boolean instead, so
every non-zero value said exhausted, and the flag decides whether four
bytes of last map hash sit between it and the resource hash. One vector
turned on the difference, because tools/gen had chosen 0x01 for the
flag when it built the plaintext:

    test/linkdata/resource-request-exhausted-short
        plaintext        01aaaaaaaa
        minimum_length   37          the mapped threshold, from 0x01

Reticulum-Go compares the byte against 0xff, as the reference does,
answered 33, and was right. The comparison is now in both programs, the
plaintext is 0xff and the vector was regenerated; its raw changed
because the plaintext did, and the pinned randomness gave it the same
initialisation vector. No other vector carries a flag byte that is
neither 0x00 nor 0xff.

This is the first defect a foreign implementation has found in the
corpus rather than the other way round, and it is the argument for
harnesses in one sentence: an implementation that disagrees with a
vector is evidence about the vector too.

Six of the seven produce the adopted signature from the private key and
the message, bit for bit, through their own signer. The seventh cannot
load an identity from a private key at all; see finding 7.

All seven agree on both forms of a delivery proof to a destination, and
all seven agree on both delivery proofs made on a link once the harness
hands them the key that signs one. Which key that is, they do not
decide: only Reticulum-Go, go-reticulum and rsReticulum have a link
proof path at all, and the other four verify no proof on a link
anywhere. See doc/link.

The channel envelope is read by two implementations of the seven.
Reticulum-Go has Channel.HandleInbound and go-reticulum Envelope.Unpack,
and both read all six bytes; rsReticulum has Envelope::unpack and reads
four of them, discarding the declared length exactly as the reference
does, so it cannot reproduce the two bytes it did not keep. The other
four define the context byte and have no code behind it.


What a result covers

Not every field came from the implementation. Six of the seven expose
no entry point that yields the parsed announce fields; their validator
returns only a boolean. For those the harness follows the
implementation's own code step by step, using its constants and its
primitives, and marks the place. Such fields test the transcription as
much as the implementation.

    implementation    derivations  announce fields  link id  signalling
    --------------------------------------------------------------------
    rsReticulum       called       called           called   called
    microReticulum    called       transcribed      called   called
    Reticulum-Go      called       transcribed      called   transcribed
    go-reticulum      called       transcribed      called   transcribed
    Reticulum-rs      called       transcribed      called   absent
    rns.js            called       transcribed      called   absent
    sgiath/reticulum  called       transcribed      called   absent

Three words, three different things. Called means the harness handed
the bytes to the implementation and printed what came back.
Transcribed means the implementation has the code and does not export
it, so the harness follows it line by line and says where. Absent means
the implementation has no such code at all, and the harness prints a
dash rather than a value borrowed from the reference. A dash fails the
vector, which is the point: see finding 14.

Derivations are the identity hash, the name hash, the destination hash,
Ed25519 verification, and for the encrypted and link vectors the key
derivation and the decryption. Verdicts are signature_valid,
hmac_valid, the plaintext, and where the implementation offers one, its
own accept or reject. All findings below rest on called code or on
source, not on transcription.

Every one of the seven derives the link id through its own code. Six
have a function for it; sgiath/reticulum has none, and its packet hash
is called instead, which is the same derivation minus the step that
belongs to links. See finding 9.

The link request proof is the one place where two implementations have
nothing to call: Reticulum-rs and sgiath/reticulum verify no proof
anywhere. For those the material is assembled as the reference
assembles it and handed to their own Ed25519 verifier, so the curve and
the encoding remain theirs.

Three of the harnesses reach past the implementation for the X25519
agreement alone, because go-reticulum, Reticulum-rs and
sgiath/reticulum keep the private key unexported or expose no exchange.
Each uses its language's standard curve, which is what those
implementations use internally.

All seven agree on the destination hash for names with at least one
aspect, the announce payload layout, the assembly of signed data
including the position of app data, Ed25519 verification, and both
forms of a delivery proof.

Every one of the seven computes the packet hash through its own
function and verifies the proof signature with its own verifier.
Reticulum-Go and go-reticulum both have a proof validator with
exported fields, so their signature_valid is that validator's own
accept or reject over the whole proof rather than a bare signature
check.

Five of the seven decrypt a packet from the reference correctly. The
encrypted vectors turned up one implementation that cannot decrypt any
of them, one that agrees on the shared secret and can derive nothing
from it because the salt is an identity hash it never computed, and one
whose ratchets do not work, all for reasons the announce vectors could
not have shown.

One of the six carries a link from request to established traffic
without a single divergence. Two cannot establish one with the
reference at all, a third establishes one and then decrypts nothing on
it, and a fourth establishes one without ever reading what was
signalled.

Reticulum-Go is a second. It establishes the link, derives the same
keys, decrypts every packet on it, and reads more of what those packets
carry than any of the six. All five of its link failures are
thresholds on a payload too short to be what its context byte said it
was: findings 22, 23, 26 and 27. Not one of them is a packet it reads
wrongly.


